@page "/docs/gateway-setup"
@attribute [Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization.Authorize]
@using ALDevToolbox.Components.Pages.Admin.Administration
@using ALDevToolbox.Components.Shared
@using ALDevToolbox.Services.Gateways
@inject GatewayBuild Build

@*
    The setup guide for the on-premises gateway (#1376): what customer IT reads
    before agreeing to install it, and uses while installing it.
    .design/on-prem-gateway.md is the source of truth; every setting, default,
    command and event id here is the gateway's own, and DocsSetupGuide tests in
    GatewaySetupDocsTests hold the settings tables to the parser and the two
    examples to "the parser accepts them".

    Named user: an IT administrator at a Business Central customer who knows
    Windows Server and their own service tiers, and nothing about AL Workbench.
    So: no class names, no AL Workbench internals, and where the design doc lists
    something as not yet tried on real servers, the guide says so or leaves it
    out rather than promising it.

    Page archetype 12 (docs / long-form), static SSR like the other docs pages.
    The download block is the Gateways page's own GatewayDownloadLink, so the
    hash and the "not code-signed yet" wording are the same in both places.
*@

<PageTitle>Gateway setup guide · AL Workbench</PageTitle>

<DocsPage Title="Setting up the on-premises gateway"
          Subtitle="For the IT team that installs it: what it does, what it needs, and how to set it up."
          Toc="@Toc"
          Prose="false">
    <article class="prose gs-article">
    <p class="prose__lead">
        The AL Workbench Gateway is a small Windows service that lets AL Workbench look after
        Business Central servers in your own network: see which versions run, install the apps
        your partner builds, import a licence and restart a service tier. It connects out to
        AL Workbench; nothing connects in. A settings file on your server, which AL Workbench
        cannot change, decides exactly what it may do.
    </p>

    <h2 id="gs-what">What the gateway does, and what it does not</h2>
    <p>
        The gateway can only carry out a fixed list of operations, and only on the service tiers
        you list in its settings file:
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Operation</th><th scope="col">What it runs</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Read the service tiers</td>
                <td>
                    State, version, tenants, installed apps, the number of open sessions and the
                    licence, every 15 minutes and after every change. Read-only.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Deploy apps</td>
                <td>
                    Publish, synchronise, install or upgrade an app, and optionally remove its
                    previous version (<code>Publish-NAVApp</code>, <code>Sync-NAVApp</code>,
                    <code>Install-NAVApp</code>, <code>Start-NAVAppDataUpgrade</code>,
                    <code>Unpublish-NAVApp</code>), then restart the tiers of that database.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Import a licence</td>
                <td><code>Import-NAVServerLicense</code>, then restart the tiers of that database.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Restart, stop or start</td>
                <td>One service tier, or every tier of one database, when someone asks from AL Workbench.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>What it never does:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>It never runs a script or a command line sent from AL Workbench.</strong>
            Every operation is a fixed script inside the program; what AL Workbench sends is
            data, such as which tier and which app. The only scripts it runs are your own
            before and after scripts, named in your settings file.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>It opens no port.</strong> It only makes outbound HTTPS calls to
            AL Workbench. No VPN and no firewall opening are needed.
        </li>
        <li>
            It does not change server configuration, companies, users or permissions, does not
            install cumulative updates or convert databases, and does not update itself.
        </li>
        <li>
            It refuses anything your settings file does not allow, whatever AL Workbench asks.
            AL Workbench can read what the file allows, but cannot change it.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Files it downloads for a deployment or a licence import are checked against the SHA-256
        AL Workbench sent with the command before they are used, and an app file is opened to
        check that its name, publisher and version are the ones AL Workbench said it was sending.
    </p>
    <div class="note note--warn">
        <span class="note__icon"><Icon Name="triangle-alert" Width="17" Height="17" /></span>
        <span class="note__title">What the settings file is protecting you from</span>
        <span class="note__body">
            If AL Workbench itself were ever compromised, it could still install any app on any
            tier your settings file allows deployments to, within the hours you allow. The file is
            the real limit: which tiers, what each may do, and when. If that is not enough, require
            signed apps on your production tiers (<code>allow_unsigned = false</code>) and list the
            certificates you trust in <code>signers</code>. A valid signature from anyone else is
            then refused.
        </span>
    </div>

    <h3 id="gs-what-log">What it records on your server</h3>
    <p>
        Every operation is logged locally when it starts and when it ends, with who asked for it
        in AL Workbench and how it went, in the Windows event log (Application, source
        <strong>AL Workbench Gateway</strong>) and in a file per day in
        <code>%ProgramData%\AL Workbench Gateway\logs</code>, kept for 30 days.
        <a href="#gs-logs">Logs and event ids</a> lists what to filter on.
    </p>

    <h2 id="gs-requirements">What you need</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>A Windows server in the network</strong> where it can reach the service tiers
            it will manage: one of the Business Central servers itself, or another server next to
            them. 64-bit Windows Server 2016 or later. The program is built on .NET 10 and carries
            its own copy of it, so nothing else needs installing; a Windows version that .NET 10
            does not support will not run it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Windows PowerShell 5.1</strong>, which comes with Windows Server 2016 and later.
            Stopping and starting service tiers, and your own before and after scripts, always run
            in it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>PowerShell 7</strong> as well, if you have newer Business Central versions. The
            gateway runs each tier's Business Central administration module in the PowerShell it
            expects that version to need: Windows PowerShell 5.1 for older versions, PowerShell 7
            from Business Central 24, and 5.1 when it cannot tell. It looks for PowerShell 7 in
            <code>C:\Program Files\PowerShell\7\pwsh.exe</code>, where its installer puts it.
            Where the version it picks does not suit a tier, set <code>powershell</code> for that
            tier in the settings file; <code>check</code> (below) shows whether the module loads.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Business Central 14 or later</strong> for deployments; they are for AL apps.
            Older servers can at most be read.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Outbound HTTPS</strong> (port 443) from the gateway's server to the AL Workbench
            address, the one in the register command your AL Workbench Admin gives you. The gateway
            uses the computer's proxy settings (WinHTTP or Internet Options, or the
            <code>HTTPS_PROXY</code> environment variable), signs in to a proxy that asks with the
            service account's own credentials, and checks certificates with Windows' certificate
            store, so a proxy that inspects TLS works once your company's root certificate is
            trusted there.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>A correct clock.</strong> Every request the gateway makes is signed and valid
            for 60 seconds, so a server clock that is far off is refused. Keep the server in time
            with your domain or an internet time source.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>For tiers on other computers:</strong> remote service control (remote
            procedure call and file sharing reachable inside your network), or PowerShell
            remoting (WinRM) if you choose that method. See <a href="#gs-account">the service
            account</a>.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="gs-account">The service account</h2>
    <p>
        By default the service runs as its own <strong>virtual account</strong>,
        <code>NT SERVICE\ALWorkbenchGateway</code>: no password to manage, not an administrator,
        and on the network it acts as the computer's own account (<code>DOMAIN\SERVERNAME$</code>).
        You can name a <strong>group managed service account</strong> instead, for example when
        the gateway must stop and start tiers on other computers as a domain identity. The account
        must be installed on the server and hold "Log on as a service". An ordinary user account
        is refused, because the gateway never stores a password.
    </p>
    <p>Whichever account you use, it needs:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>To run the Business Central administration cmdlets</strong> against each tier
            it manages - the same cmdlets your team runs in the Business Central Administration
            Shell. Exactly which rights that takes depends on your servers and version, and has not
            yet been confirmed on every Business Central version. After the gateway is approved,
            the gateway's page in AL Workbench shows whether each tier could be read, which is the
            first proof that the account has what it needs.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>The right to stop and start the Business Central service</strong>
            (<code>MicrosoftDynamicsNavServer$&lt;instance&gt;</code>) of every tier you allow it
            to restart, on this computer and on each other computer in a restart group. On another
            computer that is remote service control, or, with the
            <code>powershell-remoting</code> method, permission to connect with PowerShell
            remoting there.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Read access</strong> to the gateway's program folder, its settings file and
            your scripts - and <strong>no write access</strong> to them (see
            <a href="#gs-rights">who may change the files</a>).
        </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="note note--info">
        <span class="note__icon"><Icon Name="info" Width="17" Height="17" /></span>
        <span class="note__title">If you make the account an administrator</span>
        <span class="note__body">
            Adding the account to the local Administrators group is the quickest way to give it
            those rights, and it works. It also lets the gateway's own process change its settings
            file, which the file rules are there to prevent. If you can, grant the narrower rights
            instead.
        </span>
    </div>

    <h2 id="gs-install">Install and register it</h2>
    <p>
        You need an AL Workbench Admin for steps 4 and 5: they create the one-time registration
        code and approve the gateway once it has registered. Run every command on the gateway's
        server from a command prompt or PowerShell window opened with
        <strong>Run as administrator</strong>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="gs-download">1. Download it and check the file</h3>
    <p>
        The gateway is one file, <code>ALWorkbench.Gateway.exe</code>, for 64-bit Windows, with the
        same version as the AL Workbench it connects to.
    </p>
    @if (Build.Download is not null)
    {
        <p>Download it here, or have your AL Workbench Admin download it from Administration → Gateways:</p>
        <div class="gs-download">
            <GatewayDownloadLink IdPrefix="gs" />
        </div>
        <p>
            If the hash is different, do not run the file. The same value, in lower case, is in
            <code>ALWorkbench.Gateway.exe.sha256</code>, published beside the program.
        </p>
    }
    else
    {
        <p>
            Your AL Workbench Admin downloads it from Administration → Gateways, where its SHA-256 is
            shown with it.
        </p>
        <div class="gs-download">
            <GatewayDownloadLink IdPrefix="gs" />
        </div>
        <p>Check the SHA-256 before you run it. In PowerShell, in the folder you saved it to:</p>
        <div class="code-block">
            <div class="code-block__bar">
                <span class="code-block__lang">powershell</span>
                <span class="code-block__spacer"></span>
                <CopyButton Target="#gs-hash-command" />
            </div>
            <pre id="gs-hash-command">Get-FileHash .\ALWorkbench.Gateway.exe -Algorithm SHA256</pre>
        </div>
        <p>
            The <code>Hash</code> it prints must be exactly the SHA-256 shown with the download. If it
            is different, do not run the file.
        </p>
        <div class="note note--warn">
            <span class="note__icon"><Icon Name="triangle-alert" Width="17" Height="17" /></span>
            <span class="note__title">Not code-signed yet</span>
            <span class="note__body">
                The gateway is not code-signed yet, so Windows may say its publisher is unknown.
                Checking the SHA-256 is how you know it is the file AL Workbench published.
            </span>
        </div>
    }
    @if (Build.Download is { Signed: true })
    {
        <p>
            This release is code-signed. Check the signature too; <code>Status</code> must be
            <code>Valid</code>:
        </p>
        <div class="code-block">
            <div class="code-block__bar">
                <span class="code-block__lang">powershell</span>
                <span class="code-block__spacer"></span>
                <CopyButton Target="#gs-signature-command" />
            </div>
            <pre id="gs-signature-command">Get-AuthenticodeSignature .\ALWorkbench.Gateway.exe</pre>
        </div>
    }

    <h3 id="gs-folder">2. Put it in a folder only administrators can change</h3>
    <p>
        Create <code>C:\Program Files\AL Workbench Gateway</code> and copy
        <code>ALWorkbench.Gateway.exe</code> into it. The default rights on
        <code>C:\Program Files</code> are what the gateway needs. A folder you create elsewhere,
        such as <code>C:\ALWorkbench</code>, usually lets ordinary users add files there, and the
        gateway refuses to run from it until that is removed (see
        <a href="#gs-rights">who may change the files</a>).
    </p>

    <h3 id="gs-service">3. Install the service</h3>
    <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-block__bar">
            <span class="code-block__lang">cmd</span>
            <span class="code-block__spacer"></span>
            <CopyButton Target="#gs-install-command" />
        </div>
        <pre id="gs-install-command">cd "C:\Program Files\AL Workbench Gateway"
ALWorkbench.Gateway install</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        This creates the Windows service <strong>AL Workbench Gateway</strong>
        (<code>ALWorkbenchGateway</code>), set to start automatically (delayed) with the computer,
        and restarted by Windows if it stops on an error. It runs as the virtual account. To use a
        group managed service account instead, name it; its name ends in <code>$</code>:
    </p>
    <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-block__bar">
            <span class="code-block__lang">cmd</span>
            <span class="code-block__spacer"></span>
            <CopyButton Target="#gs-install-account-command" />
        </div>
        <pre id="gs-install-account-command">ALWorkbench.Gateway install --account CRONUS\svc-bcgateway$</pre>
    </div>
    <p class="prose__cap">
        The install uses Windows' own <code>sc.exe</code>, so <code>sc qc ALWorkbenchGateway</code>
        shows exactly what it set up. It also creates the event log source and the data folder
        <code>%ProgramData%\AL Workbench Gateway</code>, with rights only administrators can change.
    </p>

    <h3 id="gs-register">4. Register it with AL Workbench</h3>
    <p>
        The AL Workbench Admin opens <strong>Administration → Gateways</strong> and presses
        <strong>Add gateway</strong>. AL Workbench shows a command with a one-time registration
        code, valid for one hour. Run it on the server exactly as shown; it looks like this:
    </p>
    <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-block__bar">
            <span class="code-block__lang">example</span>
        </div>
        <pre>ALWorkbench.Gateway register --url https://workbench.cronus.example --code &lt;registration code&gt;</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The gateway creates its own key in the Windows key store (in the TPM when the server has
        one), which cannot be exported. Only administrators, SYSTEM and the service account can
        use it. It sends AL Workbench the public half, prints a <strong>key fingerprint</strong>,
        and starts the service. No password or shared secret is stored anywhere, and the
        registration code is useless once it has been used.
    </p>

    <h3 id="gs-approve">5. Have the Admin approve it</h3>
    <p>
        Read the key fingerprint the register command printed to the AL Workbench Admin. They see
        the same value as the gateway's <strong>check code</strong> under Administration →
        Gateways, and approve it only when the two match. Until then the gateway reports that it is
        alive but receives no work, so a code that leaked cannot connect a stranger's server.
    </p>

    <h3 id="gs-settings-step">6. Write the settings file and check it</h3>
    <p>
        Create <code>gateway.toml</code> in the program folder (<a href="#gs-policy">the settings
        file</a> below has every setting and two examples). Then run:
    </p>
    <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-block__bar">
            <span class="code-block__lang">cmd</span>
            <span class="code-block__spacer"></span>
            <CopyButton Target="#gs-check-command" />
        </div>
        <pre id="gs-check-command">ALWorkbench.Gateway check</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        It lists any mistakes in the file with their line numbers, anyone other than administrators
        who can change the gateway's files, what each tier is allowed, and asks each tier on this
        computer for its state, which proves the right PowerShell and administration module load.
        It changes nothing. The service picks up the file by itself within a minute; it does not
        need restarting. Until there is a <code>gateway.toml</code>, the gateway is registered but
        refuses everything.
    </p>
    <p class="prose__cap">
        <code>check</code> runs as you, not as the service account. If AL Workbench later says a
        tier could not be read, the service account's rights are the first thing to look at. With
        <code>check --inventory &lt;folder&gt;</code> it also saves what the gateway would report for
        each tier on this computer, one file per tier, without sending anything. The files hold the
        tiers' app lists and licence information: share them only with whoever looks after
        AL Workbench, if they ask for them.
    </p>

    <h2 id="gs-policy">The settings file</h2>
    <p>
        <code>gateway.toml</code> sits next to <code>ALWorkbench.Gateway.exe</code>. You own it:
        the gateway does only what it allows, and AL Workbench shows what it allows but cannot
        change it. It is a TOML file: text in <code>"double quotes"</code>, Windows paths in
        <code>'single quotes'</code> so the backslashes are kept, and lines starting with
        <code>#</code> are comments.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Strict.</strong> A setting, section or value the gateway does not know is a
            mistake reported with its line number, never ignored, so a typo cannot quietly widen or
            narrow what the gateway may do. Values are written exactly as listed below
            (<code>production</code>, not <code>Production</code>); computer and instance names
            are compared without regard to case, as Windows does.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Read again every minute and before every operation.</strong> No restart needed
            after an edit.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>A file with mistakes</strong> leaves the gateway reading the tiers the last
            correct version listed, and refusing every change, until it is fixed. The mistakes are
            written to the event log (event 3001) and shown in AL Workbench.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>No file</strong> means nothing is allowed; the gateway can still be registered
            and approved first.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="gs-permissions">Permissions</h3>
    <p>
        Each tier has a list of permissions, <code>allow</code>. Anything not in it is refused.
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Permission</th><th scope="col">Lets the gateway</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>inventory</code></td><td>Read the tier's state, version, tenants, apps, number of open sessions and licence.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>session_users</code></td><td>Also report <em>who</em> is signed in, not only how many. That is personal data, so leave it out unless you need it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>publish</code></td><td>Publish, synchronise, install and upgrade apps.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>unpublish</code></td><td>Remove an app's previous version after an upgrade.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>restart</code></td><td>Restart the tier as part of a deployment or a licence import.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>service_control</code></td><td>Stop, start and restart the tier on request from AL Workbench, outside a deployment. A tier stopped this way stays stopped until someone starts it, though a later deployment or licence import that restarts its database starts it again.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>licence</code></td><td>Import a licence file.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>scripts</code></td><td>Run the before and after scripts named for its database.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
        A deployment needs <code>publish</code> on the tier that publishes, <code>restart</code> on
        every tier of its database's restart group, <code>unpublish</code> when previous versions
        are removed, and <code>scripts</code> when the database names a script. A licence import
        needs <code>licence</code> on the publishing tier and <code>restart</code> on every tier of
        the group. A restart, stop or start from AL Workbench needs <code>service_control</code> on
        every tier it touches.
    </p>

    <h3 id="gs-defaults">Defaults per kind of environment</h3>
    <p>
        <code>[defaults.production]</code>, <code>[defaults.test]</code> and
        <code>[defaults.development]</code> each hold the two settings a tier of that kind gets when
        it does not set its own:
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Setting</th><th scope="col">Means</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><code>allow</code></td>
                <td>
                    The permissions, for example <code>["inventory", "publish", "restart"]</code>.
                    A tier with no <code>allow</code> of its own and none for its kind may only be
                    read (<code>["inventory"]</code>). <code>allow = []</code> means nothing at all,
                    not even reading.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>change_hours</code></td>
                <td>
                    A local time window for every permission except <code>inventory</code>, in
                    24-hour time: <code>"20:00-06:00"</code> allows changes from 20:00 until just
                    before 06:00. A start later than the end runs overnight. It is judged on this
                    server's clock, so daylight saving moves with it. Left out: changes at any
                    time. A change outside it is refused with "Outside the hours set on the
                    customer's gateway", on top of the deployment window set in AL Workbench.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
        A tier's own value replaces the default completely; the two lists are never combined. Each
        of the two settings is taken separately: a tier can set its own <code>allow</code> and still
        use its kind's <code>change_hours</code>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="gs-instance">[[instance]] - one per service tier</h3>
    <p>
        One <code>[[instance]]</code> section for each service tier the gateway may touch, at most
        200. A tier that is not listed is refused, even for reading.
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Setting</th><th scope="col">Means</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>machine</code></td><td>Required. The computer the tier runs on. A computer other than this one also needs a <a href="#gs-machine">[[machine]]</a> section.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>name</code></td><td>Required. The server instance name, as <code>Get-NAVServerInstance</code> shows it, for example <code>BC250</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>environment</code></td>
                <td>
                    <code>production</code>, <code>test</code> or <code>development</code>. Left out:
                    <code>production</code>, the most careful choice. AL Workbench shows this and
                    cannot change it. On production it asks people to confirm again before a
                    deployment or a restart, and AI assistants may never change a production tier.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr><td><code>allow</code></td><td>This tier's own permissions, replacing its kind's default.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>change_hours</code></td><td>This tier's own time window, replacing its kind's default.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>tenants</code></td><td>On a multitenant server, the tenants the gateway may act on. Left out: <code>["default"]</code>, which is right for a single-tenant server.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>publishers</code></td><td>Only publish apps from these publishers, compared without regard to case. Left out: any publisher.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>allow_unsigned</code></td><td><code>true</code> to accept apps that are not signed. Left out: <code>false</code>. Apps your partner builds in AL Workbench are not signed today, so deploying them needs this.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>signers</code></td><td>Only accept signatures from these certificates, each a thumbprint or a subject such as <code>"CN=CRONUS Partner"</code>. Left out: any signature Windows calls valid. When set, a signed app from anyone else is refused, even with <code>allow_unsigned = true</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>allow_force_sync</code></td><td><code>true</code> lets a deployment synchronise with ForceSync, which can delete tables and fields with their data. Left out: <code>false</code>, and only the normal synchronisation is allowed.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>scope</code></td><td><code>tenant</code> publishes an app for one tenant only; <code>global</code> publishes it for the whole server. Left out: <code>tenant</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>powershell</code></td><td><code>5.1</code> or <code>7</code> to choose the PowerShell for this tier's administration module. Left out: the gateway chooses from the tier's version.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="prose__cap">
        <code>tenants</code>, <code>publishers</code> and <code>signers</code> may not be empty lists:
        leave the line out instead. Requiring signed apps relies on Windows checking an app's
        signature through a component Business Central installs. That has not yet been tried on
        every Business Central version; where Windows cannot check it, the app is refused, never
        published unchecked.
    </p>

    <h3 id="gs-database">[[database]] - tiers that share a database</h3>
    <p>
        When several tiers use one database, apps are published on one of them and all of them are
        restarted afterwards, so nobody keeps running the old code. A licence import works the same
        way, because a tier reads a new licence only when it starts. A tier that no database names
        is restarted on its own after a deployment, and cannot import a licence.
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Setting</th><th scope="col">Means</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>name</code></td><td>Required. Your name for the database, shown in AL Workbench, for example <code>"CRONUS-SQL/BC250"</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>publish_on</code></td><td>Required. The tier that publishes apps and imports licences: <code>{ machine = "CRONUS-BC01", instance = "BC250" }</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>restart</code></td>
                <td>
                    The tiers restarted after an install or a licence import, in this order, each
                    written like <code>publish_on</code>. Left out: only the publishing tier. Only
                    these tiers are ever restarted, whatever AL Workbench asks, so list every tier
                    that uses the database: one left out keeps running the old version for everyone
                    signed in to it.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>restart_mode</code></td>
                <td>
                    <code>stop-all-then-start-all</code> (the default) stops every tier, then starts
                    every tier, so old and new code never run side by side.
                    <code>one-by-one</code> restarts each tier before the next, so one is always up,
                    and stops at the first tier that does not come back. The gateway waits up to
                    5 minutes for a tier to stop and 10 for it to start; every tier it stopped is
                    sent a start, even when something failed.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>shared_restart</code></td>
                <td>
                    For tiers shared by several customers, such as a multitenant server.
                    <code>true</code> allows restarting them for one customer although that signs
                    out the others' users too. Left out: <code>false</code>, and AL Workbench
                    refuses a restart, deployment or licence import whose restart would reach tiers
                    it has linked to more than one customer.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>licence_target</code></td>
                <td>
                    Where licences go, as <code>Import-NAVServerLicense -Database</code>:
                    <code>NavDatabase</code> (the application database), <code>Master</code> (the
                    master database, for every tenant), <code>Default</code> (the server's default
                    database) or <code>Tenant</code> (only the tenant AL Workbench is linked to; the
                    tiers still restart for every tenant on them). Left out: licence imports are
                    refused.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr><td><code>before</code></td><td>Optional. The full path of your own PowerShell script, run before a deployment publishes anything. See <a href="#gs-scripts">scripts</a>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>after</code></td><td>Optional. The full path of your own PowerShell script, run after the tiers have restarted.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="prose__cap">A tier belongs to one database only, and every tier a database names needs its own <code>[[instance]]</code>.</p>

    <h3 id="gs-machine">[[machine]] - other computers</h3>
    <p>
        One <code>[[machine]]</code> section for every computer, other than the gateway's own, that
        has a tier listed. The gateway's own computer must not have one.
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Setting</th><th scope="col">Means</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>name</code></td><td>Required. The computer's name, as in the tiers' <code>machine</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>method</code></td>
                <td>
                    Required. <code>service-manager</code> stops and starts the tier's Windows
                    service through Windows' remote service control; <code>powershell-remoting</code>
                    does it with PowerShell remoting (WinRM), which must be enabled on that computer.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="prose__cap">
        On another computer the gateway only stops and starts the Business Central service. It reads,
        publishes and imports licences only on the tiers of its own computer, so AL Workbench lists a
        tier on another computer that allows <code>inventory</code> as not read, saying why.
    </p>

    <h3 id="gs-example-single">Example: one server</h3>
    <p>
        The gateway runs on CRONUS-BC01, which has a production tier and a test tier. Production
        takes deployments and licences overnight only; test also allows stopping and starting, at
        any time. Each tier
        has its own database, so each has a <code>[[database]]</code> section for its licence
        target.
    </p>
    <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-block__bar">
            <span class="code-block__name">gateway.toml</span>
            <span class="code-block__spacer"></span>
            <CopyButton Target="#gs-example-single-toml" />
        </div>
        <pre id="gs-example-single-toml"># Production: deployments, restarts and licences, overnight only.
[defaults.production]
allow = ["inventory", "publish", "unpublish", "restart", "licence"]
change_hours = "20:00-06:00"

# Test: the same, plus stopping and starting on request, at any time.
[defaults.test]
allow = ["inventory", "publish", "unpublish", "restart", "service_control", "licence"]

[[instance]]
machine = "CRONUS-BC01"
name = "BC250"
environment = "production"
allow_unsigned = true

[[instance]]
machine = "CRONUS-BC01"
name = "BC250TEST"
environment = "test"
allow_unsigned = true

[[database]]
name = "CRONUS-SQL/BC250"
publish_on = { machine = "CRONUS-BC01", instance = "BC250" }
licence_target = "NavDatabase"

[[database]]
name = "CRONUS-SQL/BC250TEST"
publish_on = { machine = "CRONUS-BC01", instance = "BC250TEST" }
licence_target = "NavDatabase"</pre>
    </div>

    <h3 id="gs-example-group">Example: several tiers on one database</h3>
    <p>
        Two production tiers, on CRONUS-BC01 and CRONUS-BC02, share one database. The gateway runs
        on CRONUS-BC01 and publishes there; both tiers are stopped, then both started. Your own
        script runs before each deployment (to take a backup, say) and after the restart.
    </p>
    <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-block__bar">
            <span class="code-block__name">gateway.toml</span>
            <span class="code-block__spacer"></span>
            <CopyButton Target="#gs-example-group-toml" />
        </div>
        <pre id="gs-example-group-toml">[defaults.production]
change_hours = "20:00-06:00"

[[instance]]
machine = "CRONUS-BC01"        # this computer
name = "BC250"
environment = "production"
allow = ["inventory", "publish", "unpublish", "restart", "licence", "scripts"]
publishers = ["CRONUS Partner"]
allow_unsigned = true

[[instance]]
machine = "CRONUS-BC02"
name = "BC250"
environment = "production"
allow = ["inventory", "restart"]   # restarted with the database, never published to

[[database]]
name = "CRONUS-SQL/BC250"
publish_on = { machine = "CRONUS-BC01", instance = "BC250" }
restart = [
  { machine = "CRONUS-BC01", instance = "BC250" },
  { machine = "CRONUS-BC02", instance = "BC250" },
]
restart_mode = "stop-all-then-start-all"
licence_target = "NavDatabase"
before = 'C:\Program Files\AL Workbench Gateway\scripts\before-deploy.ps1'
after = 'C:\Program Files\AL Workbench Gateway\scripts\after-restart.ps1'

[[machine]]
name = "CRONUS-BC02"
method = "service-manager"</pre>
    </div>
    <p class="prose__cap">
        The program also comes with <code>gateway.example.toml</code>, the same settings with a
        comment on each, published with every release beside the program.
    </p>

    <h3 id="gs-scripts">Before and after scripts</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>
            Written as the full path of a <code>.ps1</code> file on a local drive
            (<code>C:\...</code>), not a share, with no <code>[</code> or <code>]</code> in it.
            The tier that publishes needs <code>scripts</code> in its <code>allow</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
            They run in Windows PowerShell 5.1 (<code>powershell.exe -File</code>) as the service
            account, for up to 30 minutes, on the gateway's computer. Every line they print goes to
            the deployment's log in AL Workbench. AL Workbench cannot send, change or choose them.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>A script fails only when it says so:</strong> <code>exit 1</code> (any code
            other than 0), an error that stops it, or the time limit. A program's exit code does not
            count on its own: in Windows PowerShell a script that ends normally exits with 0, so a
            <code>robocopy</code> at the end that returns 1 ("files copied") does not fail it. To
            fail on a program's result, end with <code>exit $LASTEXITCODE</code> or check it yourself.
        </li>
        <li>
            A failing before script stops the deployment before anything is published. A failing
            after script marks the deployment failed with the apps installed and the tiers
            restarted. No scripts run for a licence import, or for a restart asked for on its own.
        </li>
        <li>
            AL Workbench shows a script's failure message cut to 2,000 characters; the whole output
            is in the deployment's log.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="gs-rights">Who may change the files</h3>
    <p>
        Whoever can change the gateway's program, its settings file or a script it runs can run
        code as a Business Central administrator. So the gateway checks, when it starts and before
        every operation, that these are owned by Administrators, SYSTEM or TrustedInstaller, and
        that nobody else can change them - <strong>not even the gateway's own service
        account</strong>:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>The program folder and everything in it, including <code>gateway.toml</code>.</li>
        <li>Each script and its folder, and nobody else may rename or delete anything in the folders above it, up to the root of the drive (creating new folders, which a drive's root allows everyone, is fine).</li>
        <li>The install folder of each Business Central tier whose administration module it loads.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        If the program folder, the settings file or a script can be changed by someone else, the
        gateway refuses every operation and logs event 3003 naming who, and a gateway that is
        starting stops; Windows starts it again after a minute, and it carries on by itself once
        the rights are fixed. If a tier's install folder can be changed by someone else, the gateway
        refuses to run anything against that tier. The default rights on
        <code>C:\Program Files</code> pass; a folder you created yourself usually needs the
        "Users" rights to create files removed. <code>check</code> shows exactly which entries are
        the problem.
    </p>

    <h2 id="gs-update">Updating it</h2>
    <p>
        The gateway does not update itself. When AL Workbench is updated, its Gateways page marks an
        older gateway <strong>Out of date</strong>; it keeps working, but update it when you can. A
        gateway marked <strong>Unsupported</strong> is refused until it is updated. On the gateway's
        server, as an administrator:
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li><a href="#gs-download">Download the new <code>ALWorkbench.Gateway.exe</code> and check its SHA-256.</a></li>
        <li>Stop the service: <code>sc stop ALWorkbenchGateway</code> (or Services, "AL Workbench Gateway").</li>
        <li>Replace <code>ALWorkbench.Gateway.exe</code> in its folder with the new file.</li>
        <li>Start it: <code>sc start ALWorkbenchGateway</code>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
        The registration, its key, the settings file and the gateway's state are kept, so there is
        nothing to approve again. An operation that was running when you stopped it is reported to
        AL Workbench as interrupted and is not run again. Stopping waits for any service tier the
        gateway had stopped to be started again, which can take several minutes.
    </p>

    <h2 id="gs-uninstall">Removing it</h2>
    <div class="code-block">
        <div class="code-block__bar">
            <span class="code-block__lang">cmd</span>
            <span class="code-block__spacer"></span>
            <CopyButton Target="#gs-uninstall-command" />
        </div>
        <pre id="gs-uninstall-command">cd "C:\Program Files\AL Workbench Gateway"
ALWorkbench.Gateway uninstall</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        This stops and removes the service, deletes the gateway's key from the Windows key store,
        its registration and its state, and removes the event log source. It keeps the log files in
        <code>%ProgramData%\AL Workbench Gateway\logs</code>; delete them when you no longer need
        them, then delete the program folder with <code>gateway.toml</code>. Ask the AL Workbench
        Admin to <strong>revoke</strong> the gateway under Administration → Gateways, so it is
        shown as gone there too.
    </p>

    <h2 id="gs-offline">When it is offline or revoked</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Offline.</strong> The gateway tells AL Workbench it is alive every minute. After
            three missed minutes it is shown as offline, and the Admins are notified, and again when
            it is back. Nothing waits for it: an operation for an offline gateway fails with "The
            gateway was offline" once its time is up (15 minutes after it was due, or the end of its
            deployment window, whichever is earlier), so a deployment never starts after its window.
            AL Workbench keeps showing what the gateway last reported.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Cannot reach AL Workbench.</strong> The gateway keeps trying, waiting a little
            longer each time up to five minutes, and logs event 2003, then 2004 when it is through.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Stopped in the middle of an operation.</strong> When it starts again it tells
            AL Workbench the operation was interrupted, and never runs it again: a person decides
            whether to deploy again. Before it takes any new work it starts every service tier it had
            stopped and not yet started again (events 1010, 1011, and 1012 if one would not start -
            start that one by hand).
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Revoked.</strong> An Admin can revoke a gateway in AL Workbench at any time. Its
            unfinished operations fail at once, and everything it asks afterwards is refused. The
            service keeps running and logs event 2005 about every five minutes, so you can see why.
            To connect it again, the Admin chooses <strong>Re-register</strong> to get a new code;
            run the register command again and have them approve the new fingerprint. Otherwise
            remove it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Clock far off.</strong> Refused the same way as revoked (event 2005, saying it is
            the time). Correct the clock; the gateway carries on by itself.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Too old.</strong> A gateway AL Workbench no longer accepts logs event 2009 with
            the version it needs. <a href="#gs-update">Update it</a>.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="gs-logs">Logs and event ids</h2>
    <p>
        The Windows event log (Application, source <strong>AL Workbench Gateway</strong>) and the
        daily files in <code>%ProgramData%\AL Workbench Gateway\logs</code>, kept 30 days, hold the
        same lines. Each operation gets a line when it starts and when it ends, with who asked for
        it in AL Workbench and the outcome.
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Event</th><th scope="col">Means</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>1000, 1001, 1002</td><td>An operation started, succeeded, failed.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>1003</td><td>An operation was refused, usually by the settings file, with the reason. Nothing was changed.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>1004</td><td>An operation was cut short when the gateway last stopped, and reported as interrupted.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>1005</td><td>A result that had not reached AL Workbench before the gateway stopped was sent after it started.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>1006 to 1009</td><td>Reading the tiers: sent, could not be sent, one tier not taken, nothing read because the settings file is missing or broken.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>1010, 1011, 1012</td><td>Tiers left stopped when the gateway stopped: found, started again, could not be started again (start it by hand).</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2000, 2001, 2002</td><td>Registered, waiting for approval, approved.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2003, 2004</td><td>Cannot reach AL Workbench, reached it again.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2005</td><td>AL Workbench refused the gateway: revoked, or this computer's clock is far off.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2006</td><td>Not registered yet.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2007</td><td>Stopped on an unexpected error; Windows restarts it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2008</td><td>The rights on the data folder were changed, so it will not run. Run <code>install</code> again to put them back.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2009</td><td>This version is too old for AL Workbench.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>3000 to 3003</td><td>The settings file: read, has mistakes, missing, can be changed by someone other than administrators.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="prose__cap">
        A deployment's full log, line by line, is also kept with the deployment in AL Workbench, where
        the people who asked for it can read it.
    </p>
